// Cs3.grammar — C# 3.0: lambda expressions (T3.2.1).
//
// Lambdas are EXPRESSIONS (Roslyn ParseLambdaExpression, LanguageParser.cs:13891), added to Primary
// (append, the same pattern Cs2 uses for AnonymousMethod and Cs11 for raw strings). A lambda is:
//   [params] "=>" (Expression | Block)
// where params is a simple (unparenthesized) identifier OR a parenthesized (possibly empty) list of
// typed/untyped parameters, and the body is an expression OR a block (disambiguated by the leading
// `{`). CS3 only: no async (CS5), no static (CS8), no ref/out/params/in/readonly params
// (CS7.2+/CS8/CS9), no explicit return type, no attributed lambdas.
//
// `=>` TOKENIZATION: the fat arrow is a SINGLE literal (no new terminal). CSharpTerminals.cs defines
// NO multi-char operator terminals — operators are grammar LITERALS matched directly against the raw
// input (Literal.TryMatch, ExtensibleParser/Rules.cs:76, a StartsWith on the text). Multi-char
// operators already work as single literals in Cs1: OperatorSymbol has "==" / "!=" / "++" / "--"
// (Cs1:368-371) and the Expression rule uses "<=" / ">=" / "&&" / "||" (Cs1:656-670). So "=>" matches
// the 2-char sequence directly; there is no `=`-then-`>` split. Roslyn confirms `=>` is one token
// (SyntaxKind.EqualsGreaterThanToken, LanguageParser.cs:13919/13934).
//
// Mutual exclusivity (longest-match, no equal-length tie):
//   * lambda vs plain identifier: `x => ...` — IdentifierName matches `x` (stops before "=>");
//     LambdaExpression matches `x => ...` (longer). Longest-match -> lambda. A bare `x` (no "=>") ->
//     IdentifierName only (LambdaExpression fails: no "=>"). No tie.
//   * lambda vs parenthesized expression: `(x) => ...` — Parens matches `(x)` (stops after ")");
//     LambdaExpression matches `(x) => ...` (longer). Longest-match -> lambda. A bare `(x)` (no "=>")
//     -> Parens only (LambdaExpression fails: no "=>"). No tie.
//   * lambda body expression vs block: `{` -> Block (Expression fails: "{" is not an expression
//     start); else -> Expression (Block fails: no "{"). Different leading tokens, mutually exclusive,
//     no tie.
//
// TDOPP body stops at separators: the expression body is a full TDOPP Expression (minPrecedence 0).
// It STOPS at ")" and "}" (neither is a binary operator, so no postfix applies). It DOES consume a
// following "," (the Comma postfix, binding power 1, is applicable at minPrecedence 0) — this is the
// EXISTING grammar behavior for any top-level Expression in an argument list (e.g. `new Foo(1, 2)`
// parses `1, 2` as one comma expression), not a lambda-specific defect, and it does not cause a parse
// failure for the positive cases (the whole input is still consumed). See the progress doc.
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Parser/LanguageParser.cs):
//   ParseLambdaExpression (13891) -> worker (13903): "(" -> ParseLambdaParameterList +
//   EqualsGreaterThanToken + ParseLambdaBody; else identifier + EqualsGreaterThanToken + ParseLambdaBody.
//   ParseLambdaBody (13945): "{" -> ParseBlock, else ParsePossibleRefExpression.
//   ParseLambdaParameterList (13950): "(" (ParseLambdaParameter,)* ")" (requireOneElement:false).
//   ParseLambdaParameter (14007): modifiers (ref/out/params/in/readonly — version-gated, not CS3) +
//   optional type (ShouldParseLambdaParameterType 14038) + identifier.
//   IsPossibleLambdaExpression (13054): the disambiguation checks PeekToken(1) == EqualsGreaterThanToken.
//   Syntax tests: ExpressionParsingTests.cs:2039 (a => b), :2075 (a => { }), :2095 (() => b),
//   :2135 (() => { }), :2157 ((a) => b), :2181 ((a, a2) => b), :2208 ((T a) => b).
//
// CS3-only: no async (CS5), no static (CS8), no ref/out params (CS7.2/CS9), no in/readonly params
// (CS7.2/CS8), no params keyword (CS9), no explicit return type, no attributed lambdas.

// === Lambda expression (C# 3.0). Re-declares Primary (append, as Cs2 does for AnonymousMethod and
// Cs11 for raw strings). `=>` is a single literal (see the `=>` tokenization note above). ===
Primary =
    | LambdaExpression = LambdaParameters "=>" LambdaBody;

// Lambda parameters: a simple (unparenthesized) true identifier OR a parenthesized (possibly empty)
// list. Named union (the meta-grammar forbids `|` inside a group). Longest-match disambiguation:
// `x` -> SimpleLambdaParameter (LambdaParameterList fails: no "("); `(x)` -> LambdaParameterList
// (SimpleLambdaParameter fails: "(" is not an identifier). Roslyn ParseLambdaExpression (13916-13941).
LambdaParameters =
    | SimpleLambdaParameter = !ReservedKeyword Identifier
    | LambdaParameterList = "(" (LambdaParameter; ",")* ")";

// A single lambda parameter (CS3): explicitly typed (`int x`) OR an untyped name (`x`). No modifiers
// (ref/out/params/in/readonly are CS7.2+/CS8/CS9 — Roslyn ParseLambdaParameter 14007, version-gated).
// Named union. Longest-match: `int x` -> typed (untyped fails: int is reserved, not a TypeName);
// `x` -> untyped (typed fails: needs a 2nd name); `Foo x` -> typed (longer than the untyped `Foo`).
LambdaParameter =
    | LambdaTypedParameter = Type TypeName
    | LambdaUntypedParameter = TypeName;

// Lambda body: an expression OR a block. Disambiguated by the leading token: `{` -> Block, else ->
// Expression (Roslyn ParseLambdaBody 13945). A `{` is not an expression start (no Primary alternative
// begins with `{`), so the two alternatives are mutually exclusive (no tie).
LambdaBody =
    | Expression
    | Block;

// === T3.2.2 — C# 3.0 object/collection initializers + anonymous types. ===
// Re-declares NewBody (append, the same pattern as the Primary re-declaration above and Cs2's
// AnonymousMethod). The initializer `{ ... }` is a NEW construct that follows an object creation
// (or a bare `new`). The Cs1 NewBody (Cs1.grammar:735) requires `[` (array rank) or `(` (argument
// list) after the NewBaseType, so `new C { ... }` REJECTS at Cs1 (verified in T2.3.1). Each of the
// two re-declared alternatives REQUIRES the new construct (REQUIRED-new-construct principle):
//   * ObjectCreationWithInitializer REQUIRES the Initializer (the Cs1 ObjectCreation requires `(`
//     and has no initializer). Longest-match: `new C(1) { ... }` -> this (longer than the Cs1 `C(1)`);
//     `new C(1)` (no `{`) -> Cs1 only (this fails: no Initializer). No equal-length tie (one needs a
//     trailing `{`, the other does not).
//   * AnonymousType REQUIRES a `{` DIRECTLY after `new` (no type name) -> `new { ... }`; mutually
//     exclusive with every NewBaseType-based alternative (which require a type name first).
// Roslyn: ParseNewExpression (LanguageParser.cs:13245) dispatches IsAnonymousType (13318, `new` + `{`)
// to ParseAnonymousTypeExpression (13323); otherwise ParseArrayOrObjectCreationExpression (13383)
// parses an optional argument list (13406) + an optional object/collection initializer (13411-13414).

NewBody =
    | ObjectCreationWithInitializer = NewBaseType ArgumentList? Initializer
    | AnonymousType = AnonymousInitializer;

// The initializer: `{` + comma-separated elements + `}`. Empty `{ }` is allowed (Roslyn
// ParseObjectOrCollectionInitializer accepts an empty list; `new C { }` is valid C#). The element
// list is a SeparatedList with the default (Forbidden) trailing-separator behavior, so a trailing
// comma (`new C { X = 5, }`) is rejected.
Initializer = "{" (InitializerElement; ",")* "}";

// A single initializer element: EITHER a member assignment (`Identifier = Expression`, a SINGLE `=`)
// OR a plain expression (a collection element). The two alternatives are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE (no
// equal-length tie) — see the `=` vs `==` note below.
//
// `=` vs `==` disambiguation (the critical finding):
//   * A grammar Literal is a StartsWith match (Rules.cs:76): Literal("=") WOULD match the first `=`
//     of `==`. So a bare `Identifier "=" Expression` would mis-read `x == 5` (consuming `x =` and
//     leaving `= 5`, which is not an Expression -> it backtracks, but the disambiguation must be
//     explicit, not accidental).
//   * Roslyn disambiguates with IsNamedAssignment (LanguageParser.cs:13368): IsTrueIdentifier() &&
//     PeekToken(1).Kind == EqualsToken. Because Roslyn tokenizes `==` as ONE token (EqualsEqualsToken),
//     PeekToken(1) after `x` in `x == 5` is EqualsEqualsToken (not EqualsToken) -> NOT a named
//     assignment -> parsed as an expression. The `!("==")` lookahead replicates this exactly:
//       - `X = 5`  -> `!("==")` passes (next is `= `), `"="` matches -> MemberAssignment.
//       - `x == 5` -> `!("==")` fails (next is `==`) -> MemberAssignment fails -> Expression.
//   * The SECOND alternative is guarded with `!(Identifier !("==") "=")` so it matches ONLY when the
//     input is not a member-assignment start. This makes the two alternatives mutually exclusive:
//       for `X = 5` only MemberAssignment matches; for `x == 5` / `5` only the Expression alternative
//     matches. Without this guard, `X = 5` would match BOTH MemberAssignment and Expression (the
//     Assign operator, Cs1.grammar:672) at the same length -> an equal-length tie.
InitializerElement =
    | MemberAssignment = Identifier !("==") "=" Expression
    | NotMemberAssignment = !(Identifier !("==") "=") Expression;

// Anonymous type: `new { X = 5, Y = 6 }`. REQUIRES at least one element (`+`): `new { }` (empty
// anonymous type) is NOT valid in CS3. Roslyn's PARSER accepts the empty list (requireOneElement:
// false, LanguageParser.cs:13338) but the BINDER rejects it (CS0659 "Anonymous type must have at
// least one member"); we enforce that binder rule at the parse level (documented deviation).
AnonymousInitializer = "{" (InitializerElement; ",")+ "}";

// ============================================================================
// T3.2.3 — C# 3.0 auto-properties (class/struct only).
//
// The Cs1 concrete class/struct property (Cs1.grammar:188) is:
//   Property = Attributes? PropertyModifier* Type TypeName "{" AccessorDeclaration+ "}" ";"?
// with AccessorDeclaration = AccessorName Block (Cs1.grammar:204) — EVERY accessor REQUIRES a block
// body. C# 1.0 has NO auto-properties, so `class C { int P { get; set; } }` REJECTS at Cs1 (T2.1.2
// Property_AutoPropertyInClass_Fails). The INTERFACE property (InterfaceProperty, Cs1.grammar:209) and
// the ABSTRACT property (AbstractProperty, Cs1.grammar:194) are SEPARATE rules (both use
// InterfaceAccessor = AccessorName ";") and are NOT re-declared here — the interface auto-accessor form
// (`interface I { int P { get; set; } }`) stays a C# 1.0 feature.
//
// APPROACH: re-declare `Property` (append, T0.3 merge) with an alternative that REQUIRES at least one
// AUTO (semicolon) accessor (the REQUIRED-new-construct) and allows an optional `= Expression`
// initializer. The Cs1 alternative requires ALL-block accessors; the Cs3 alternative requires >= one
// auto accessor. The two are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE (a property is either all-block or has an auto
// accessor) — never an equal-length tie. Re-declaring `Property` (rather than `AccessorDeclaration`)
// is what avoids the tie: re-declaring AccessorDeclaration to add a `;` form would make the Cs1
// `AccessorDeclaration+` also match the all-block case → tie.
//
// Accessor forms (Roslyn ParseAccessorDeclaration LanguageParser.cs:4632-4729): block (`get { }`,
// 4683-4687), semicolon/auto (`get;`, 4688-4690), arrow (`get =>`, 4683, CS7 — excluded). A MIX of
// block and auto accessors is parsed naturally (Roslyn ParseAccessorList 4375-4406 loops per accessor)
// — the auto-getter (`get; set { }`) and auto-setter (`get { } set;`) forms. C# properties require a
// `get` (binder CS0858), so the accessor list is get-first (set-first / setter-only not modeled).
//
// Property initializer `= Expression` (Roslyn ParsePropertyDeclaration 4289-4295: `= ParseVariableInitializer`).
// Roslyn gates the auto-property INITIALIZER at CS6 (IDS_FeatureAutoPropertyInitializer -> CSharp6,
// MessageID.cs:686); the task requires it as part of the CS3 auto-property feature, so it is attached
// to this Cs3 alternative (parses at v3, rejects at v2) — a documented deviation.
//
// CS3 only: no `init` (CS9, MessageID.cs:601), no `required` (CS11, MessageID.cs:549), no
// expression-bodied accessors `get =>` (CS7, MessageID.cs:679), no backing-field `field` access (CS8).
//
// Roslyn syntax tests: DeclarationParsingTests.cs TestClassProperty (4158, `class a { b c { get; set; } }`),
// TestClassAutoPropertyWithInitializer (4448, `class a { b c { get; set; } = d; }`).

// === T3.2.3: re-declared concrete class/struct property (auto-property). The Cs3 alternative
// REQUIRES an auto (semicolon) accessor via AutoPropertyAccessorList; the optional initializer
// `("= Expression)?` is part of this alternative. Mutually exclusive with the Cs1 all-block
// alternative (no equal-length tie). ===
Property = Attributes? PropertyModifier* Type TypeName "{" AutoPropertyAccessorList "}" ("=" Expression)? ";"?;

// Auto-property accessor list: at least one auto (semicolon) accessor, get-first (C# properties
// require a `get`). Named union (the meta-grammar forbids `|` inside a group). The four shapes are
// disambiguated by longest-match (a longer shape wins when it matches); none has an equal-length tie
// with another (each pair differs in a leading token or in length).
AutoPropertyAccessorList =
    | AutoGetterOnly        = "get" ";"
    | AutoGetSet            = "get" ";" "set" ";"
    | BlockGetterAutoSetter = "get" Block "set" ";"
    | AutoGetterBlockSetter = "get" ";" "set" Block;

// ============================================================================
// T3.2.4 — C# 3.0 extension methods (the `this` parameter modifier).
//
// The KEY new construct is the `this` PARAMETER MODIFIER: `static void M(this int x) { }`.
// In Roslyn, `this` is a parameter modifier (IsParameterModifierExcludingScoped,
// LanguageParser.cs:4999: ThisKeyword/RefKeyword/OutKeyword/InKeyword/ParamsKeyword/ReadOnlyKeyword),
// parsed in the parameter modifier list (ParseParameterModifiers, LanguageParser.cs:5015, comment 5022
// "in/out/ref/readonly/params/this"). The parser accepts it UNCONDITIONALLY (no version gate); the
// version gate (extension methods = C# 3.0) and the first-parameter restriction are BINDER concerns
// (CheckParameterModifiers, ParameterHelpers.cs:587: IDS_FeatureExtensionMethod -> CSharp3,
// MessageID.cs:717-721; ERR_BadThisParam "not on the first parameter", ParserErrorMessageTests.cs:3472).
//
// `this` is ALREADY a Cs1 ReservedKeyword (Cs1.grammar:604) and a Cs1 Primary alternative
// (Cs1.grammar:679, the `this` EXPRESSION primary, e.g. `this.M()`). The `this` in a parameter list
// (`this int x`) is a PARAMETER MODIFIER — a DIFFERENT context from `this` as an expression primary.
// Re-declaring ParameterModifier to append `"this"` does NOT touch Primary, so the `this` expression
// primary (`class C { void M() { this.N(); } }`) is UNAFFECTED (still a C# 1.0 feature, parses at v1/v2).
//
// APPROACH (T0.3 append-merge of the Cs1 modifier unions — the same pattern as `partial` in T3.1.3):
//   * Re-declare `ParameterModifier` to ADD `"this"` (the CS3 feature). The Cs1 union is
//     `| "ref" | "out" | "params"`; the merged v3 union is `| "ref" | "out" | "params" | "this"`.
//     Each alternative is a distinct word -> no equal-length tie. `this` is a reserved keyword, so it
//     can never be a `Type`/`TypeName` (TypeName = !ReservedKeyword Identifier, Cs1.grammar:23) -> no
//     ambiguity with the parameter's type.
//   * The `static` CLASS modifier is C# 2.0 (Roslyn IDS_FeatureStaticClasses -> CSharp2, MessageID.cs:728;
//     Cs1 test Class_StaticModifier_Fails asserts `static class a { }` fails at Cs1). It is declared in
//     Cs2.grammar (ClassModifier = | "static"), NOT here. The extension method positive tests parse at v3
//     because v3 merges Cs1+Cs2+Cs3 (Cs2 supplies `static`). `static` is NOT added to StructModifier
//     (a struct cannot be static — CS0701).
//
// Mutual exclusivity (longest-match, no equal-length tie):
//   * `this int x` (v3): ParameterModifier* = `this`, Type = `int`, TypeName = `x`. The Cs1 alternative
//     (no `this`) fails at `this` (not a Cs1 modifier, not a Type) -> Cs3 only. No tie.
//   * `int x` (v3): ParameterModifier* = empty, Type = `int`, TypeName = `x` -> no `this` modifier. No tie.
//   * `static class E` (v3): ClassModifier* = `static`, "class", ... -> matches. A bare `class E` (no
//     static) -> ClassModifier* empty. Different inputs, no tie. `static` (reserved) can never be a
//     TypeName (Cs1:23) -> it is only ever a modifier, never a class name.
//   * `this` modifier vs `this` expression primary: a parameter list (`Parameter` -> `ParameterModifier`)
//     vs an expression (`Primary`) — different contexts, no conflict.
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main):
//   IsParameterModifierExcludingScoped (LanguageParser.cs:4999): ThisKeyword (5003).
//   ParseParameterModifiers (LanguageParser.cs:5015): comment 5022 "in/out/ref/readonly/params/this".
//   CheckParameterModifiers (ParameterHelpers.cs:587): ThisKeyword ->
//   Binder.CheckFeatureAvailability(IDS_FeatureExtensionMethod) (607); first-parameter (ERR_BadThisParam).
//   IDS_FeatureExtensionMethod -> CSharp3 (MessageID.cs:717-721); IDS_FeatureStaticClasses -> CSharp2
//   (MessageID.cs:728).
//   Syntax tests: DeclarationParsingTests.cs:19226 ScopedInParameter1
//   (`static class C { public static void M(scoped in this int x) { } }`); ParserErrorMessageTests.cs:3472
//   CS1107ERR_DupParamMod (malformed `this` forms are BINDER diagnostics); SymbolDisplayTests.cs:330
//   TestExtensionMethodAsStatic (canonical `public static TSource M<TSource>(this C1<TSource> source,
//   int index) {}`).
//
// CS3 only: the `this` PARAMETER MODIFIER is the CS3 feature. No extension properties or other
// extension-member forms (later versions). The `this` modifier is allowed on ANY parameter at the parse
// level (the first-parameter-only restriction is a binder concern, CS1100).

// === T3.2.4: the `this` parameter modifier (C# 3.0). Re-declares ParameterModifier (append, T0.3
// merge). The Cs1 union is `| "ref" | "out" | "params"`; the merged v3 union adds `"this"`. Distinct
// words -> no equal-length tie. `this` is a reserved keyword (Cs1:604), so it can never be a
// Type/TypeName (Cs1:23) -> no ambiguity with the parameter's type. Roslyn
// IsParameterModifierExcludingScoped (LanguageParser.cs:4999: ThisKeyword 5003). ===
ParameterModifier =
    | "this";

// NOTE (T3.2.4): the `static` CLASS modifier is C# 2.0 (Roslyn IDS_FeatureStaticClasses -> CSharp2,
// MessageID.cs:728), NOT a CS3 feature. It is declared in Cs2.grammar (ClassModifier = | "static"),
// not here. `static` is already a Cs1 MethodModifier (Cs1:293), so the `static` in `static void M(...)`
// needs no change. NOT added to StructModifier (a struct cannot be static — CS0701).
